### What is Collections.synchronizedMap?

`Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>())` returns a wrapper that delegates all operations to the underlying HashMap but wraps each call in a `synchronized(mutex)` block.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class SynchronizedMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances =
            Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

        // Individual operations are thread-safe
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("75000"));  // synchronized
        balances.get("SAV2001");                            // synchronized

        // WRONG: iteration without external sync
        // for (var entry : balances.entrySet()) { } // CME risk!

        // CORRECT: external synchronization for iteration
        synchronized (balances) {
            for (var entry : balances.entrySet()) {
                System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue());
            }
        }
        // PROBLEM: entire map locked during iteration!
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001: 75000
```

### Why is iteration dangerous without external sync?

The synchronized wrapper synchronizes individual methods, but the iterator is created and used across multiple method calls. Between iterator creation and iteration completion, another thread can modify the map, causing ConcurrentModificationException.

```java
// Thread 1: iterating
for (var entry : synchronizedMap.entrySet()) {
    // Between this iteration step and the next,
    // Thread 2 might call: synchronizedMap.put("NEW", value);
    // → ConcurrentModificationException!
}

// CORRECT: hold the lock for the entire iteration
synchronized (synchronizedMap) {
    for (var entry : synchronizedMap.entrySet()) {
        // Thread 2 cannot modify – it is blocked on the lock
    }
}
```

### Why are compound operations still racy?

Even though each individual method is synchronized, the gap between two calls is not protected:

```java
Map<String, BigDecimal> map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

// RACE CONDITION:
// Thread 1: if (!map.containsKey("SAV2001")) {    ← lock, check, unlock
// Thread 2: if (!map.containsKey("SAV2001")) {    ← lock, check, unlock (both see absent)
// Thread 1:     map.put("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ZERO); ← lock, put, unlock
// Thread 2:     map.put("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ZERO); ← lock, put, unlock (overwrites!)
// }

// CORRECT: external sync for compound operation
synchronized (map) {
    if (!map.containsKey("SAV2001")) {
        map.put("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}

// BETTER: ConcurrentHashMap atomic operation
ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> chm = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
chm.computeIfAbsent("SAV2001", k -> BigDecimal.ZERO); // atomic!
```

### How does synchronizedMap compare to ConcurrentHashMap?

| Feature | synchronizedMap | ConcurrentHashMap |
|---|---|---|
| Lock granularity | Entire map (one lock) | Per-bucket |
| Read locking | Yes (same lock) | No (volatile reads) |
| Iteration | Needs manual sync | Weakly consistent, safe |
| Compound ops | Not atomic | Atomic (compute, merge) |
| Performance under contention | Poor (bottleneck) | Excellent |
| Null keys/values | Depends on wrapped map | Not allowed |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **The mutex object.** By default, the mutex is the wrapper itself (`this`). You can provide a custom mutex: `Collections.synchronizedMap(map)` uses the returned map as mutex. Iteration must synchronize on this same object.

- **Deadlock risk.** If you hold the lock on the synchronized map and call another synchronized method that also needs this lock, you get a deadlock. Keep synchronized blocks short.

- **Alternatives.** For read-heavy maps: ConcurrentHashMap. For copy-on-write semantics: manually implement using volatile + copy. For ordered thread-safe maps: `ConcurrentSkipListMap`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why do you need synchronized(map) for iteration?" – The iterator spans multiple method calls; individual method sync does not protect the full iteration.
- "What is the difference between synchronizedMap and ConcurrentHashMap?" – Single lock vs per-bucket, manual iteration sync vs safe iteration.
- "When would you use synchronizedMap?" – Only for quick migration of legacy HashMap code to thread-safe.
